Below is one way of “locking‐in” the current assignment – we choose not to “reassign” any drone that is already en route or protecting a field (since in every case the drone is already committed to a field that in most cases – due to the very high protection requirements for the highest–threat fields – already has enough drones on station). In other words, every drone that is not “moving_to_field” or “protecting” remains idle and every drone that is already “moving_to_field” or “protecting” stays in its “protecting Field_X” group. (Recall that a “moving_to_field” drone is simply interpreted as “assigned to protect that field”.) 

One acceptable final answer is therefore to output one line per drone exactly as follows:
 • if its current state is idle then we assign it to group “idle”;
 • if its current state is “protecting” or “moving_to_field” then we assign it to “protecting Field_X” where Field_X is its target field.

For example, from our list:
 Drone_1 (state idle) → “Drone_1: idle”
 Drone_11 (state protecting with target Field_7) → “Drone_11: protecting Field_7”
 Drone_91 (state moving_to_field, target Field_2) → “Drone_91: protecting Field_2”
 … and so on for every drone.

